## Wiring Connections

### IR Sensor

| IR Sensor | ESP32 |
|-----------|--------|
| VCC | 5V |
| GND | GND |
| OUT | GPIO12 |

### Servo Motor

| Servo Wire | ESP32 |
|------------|--------|
| Red | 5V |
| Brown | GND |
| Yellow | GPIO13 |

### TM1637 Display

| TM1637 | ESP32 |
|---------|--------|
| VCC | 5V |
| GND | GND |
| DIO | GPIO16 |
| CLK | GPIO17 |

---

## Blynk Configuration

| Virtual Pin | Purpose |
|-------------|---------|
| V2 | IR Sensor Status |
| V3 | Detection Counter |
| V4 | Servo Control Slider |
| V5 | Auto/Manual Mode Switch |

### Widgets

#### Value Display (V2)
Displays IR sensor status.

#### Value Display (V3)
Displays object detection count.

#### Slider (V4)
Controls servo angle from 0° to 180°.

#### Switch (V5)
Controls Automatic and Manual modes.

---

# Task 1 – IR Sensor Reading

### Objective
Read the IR sensor and display the detection status on Blynk.

### Description

The IR sensor is connected to GPIO12.

- Object detected → Send value 1 to Blynk V2
- No object detected → Send value 0 to Blynk V2

### Result

The IR sensor status can be monitored remotely through the Blynk application.

---

# Task 2 – Servo Motor Control via Blynk

### Objective
Control the servo motor using a Blynk slider.

### Description

A slider widget on V4 sends values between 0° and 180°.

The ESP32 reads the slider value and moves the servo motor accordingly.

### Result

The servo motor can be controlled remotely using the Blynk mobile application.

---

# Task 3 – Automatic IR Servo Action

### Objective
Automatically open the gate when an object is detected.

### Description

When the IR sensor detects an object:

1. Servo rotates to 90°
2. Gate remains open for 3 seconds
3. Servo returns to 0°
4. System waits until the object leaves

### Result

The gate automatically opens and closes without user interaction.

---

# Task 4 – TM1637 Display Integration

### Objective
Count object detections and display the count.

### Description

Every detection event:

- Increases the counter by 1
- Updates the TM1637 display
- Sends the count to Blynk V3

### Result

The detection count is visible both locally and remotely.

---

# Task 5 – Manual Override Mode

### Objective
Enable or disable automatic gate operation.

### Description

A Blynk switch on V5 controls the system mode.

### Automatic Mode (V5 = 1)

- IR sensor active
- Gate opens automatically
- Counter increases
- Display updates

### Manual Mode (V5 = 0)

- IR sensor ignored
- Automatic gate control disabled

### Result

Users can switch between automatic and manual operation using Blynk.

---

## How to Run

1. Connect all hardware components.
2. Upload the Python code to the ESP32.
3. Configure WiFi credentials.
4. Configure the Blynk Auth Token.
5. Open the Blynk application.
6. Run the program.
7. Test all five tasks.

